jsonb_toaster_lite — TOAST/storage layer for jsonb
====================================================

Purpose
-------
jsonb_toaster_lite is a TOAST/storage layer for the standard
PostgreSQL jsonb type.  It uses the Pluggable TOAST API
(contrib/toastapi) to plug in custom physical storage strategies —
out-of-line plain chunks and per-chunk compressed chunks — and a
slice-aware reader so that public `jsonb -> 'key'` does not have to
detoast the whole value.

Read-side v1 scope
------------------
What is exercised in this snapshot:

  - storage modes: JBTL_PLAIN_JSONB (small inline), JBTL_POINTER
    (out-of-line plain chunks), JBTL_POINTER_COMPRESSED_CHUNKS
    (per-chunk pglz compressed);
  - public `jsonb -> 'key'` reaches the JBTL slice fast path through
    the Toastapi_jsonb_object_field_hook callback, before
    PG_GETARG_JSONB_P would otherwise force a full detoast;
  - slice fetch over POINTER and POINTER_COMPRESSED_CHUNKS modes;
  - full detoast fallback for everything the fast path declines.

The public read path therefore avoids full-detoast for the keys it
handles.  Correctness is preserved on the values it declines: the
hook returns false and core falls through to its existing
jsonb_object_field body.

Out of scope for this snapshot
------------------------------
  - writer policy is unchanged from the prior published patch.
    tsr_toast continues to emit JBTL_PLAIN_JSONB for small values;
    no writer policy work is bundled with the read-side cleanup;
  - update / DIFF / SUBTREE behavior (POINTER_DIFF, POINTER_DIFF_COMP,
    POINTER_SUBTREE) is deferred.  The C code that supports those
    modes (jbtl_update, jbtl_try_spill_subtree, the DIFF and SUBTREE
    pointer encoders, the subtree_refs catalog runtime) is carried in
    the tree and is reachable through tsr_update / tsr_toast, but is
    NOT validated by the read-side v1 regression suite, and the
    remaining open boundaries (DIFF-on-DIFF rebase, fallback
    amplification, rollback model) are still pending focused work;
  - update counters (jbtl_update_calls, _reset, _diffs_emitted) and
    SUBTREE maintenance SQL (jbtl_subtree_refs_check, _gc) and SUBTREE
    test helpers (jbtl_test_*) are kept in the SQL surface as
    deferred update-side material;
  - `->>` (jsonb_object_field_text) does NOT yet have its own dispatch
    hook.  Reads via `->>` go through the existing core path with full
    detoast.  Adding a `->>` dispatch hook is a separate piece of work;
  - no file or extension rename has been performed.  The names
    jsonb_toaster_lite / jbtl_ / JBTL_ are retained.

Source provenance
-----------------
TOAST internals (chunk machinery, pointer constructors, sliced
detoast iterator, diff applier) were originally ported from
postgrespro/postgres@jsonb_toaster (contrib/jsonb_toaster) under this
extension's own jbtl_* / Jbtl* / JBTL_* symbol namespace.  Original
symbol names from the postgrespro branch are referenced only in
port-trace comments where useful.

This extension does not introduce any symbol from the postgrespro
generic-jsonb (jsonx_*) dispatch family.  All jsonb construction,
traversal, and equality is handled by the standard core API
(utils/jsonb.h).  The KVMap value-size sorting feature lives in core
src/backend/utils/adt/jsonb_util.c and is consumed via the public
utils/jsonb.h API only.

Read-side v1 regression
-----------------------
make installcheck (after CREATE EXTENSION toastapi; CREATE EXTENSION
jsonb_toaster_lite) runs:

  jsonb_toaster_lite                 basic install + INSERT/SELECT
  jsonb_toaster_lite_roundtrip       text round-trip correctness
  jsonb_toaster_lite_slice_plain     slice fetch on POINTER mode
                                     with chunks_fetched assertions
  jsonb_toaster_lite_slice_compressed slice fetch on POINTER_COMPRESSED_CHUNKS
  jsonb_toaster_lite_object_field    public `->` fast path correctness
                                     plus chunks_fetched / fallback
                                     assertions
  jsonb_toaster_lite_nondefault_schema non-default schema install/usage

The probes jbtl_slice_probe and jbtl_object_field_probe are kept as
temporary validation SQL (alongside jbtl_object_field,
jbtl_object_field_text, and jbtl_chunk_inspect) because the
chunks_fetched / fallback assertions distinguish "fast path was
taken" from mere correctness.  These will be archived once the
read-side v1 surface ships and a permanent equivalent is in place.
